<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS COMMENT SITS INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT DELIBERATELY. A comment placed ABOVE
        <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 2026-08-20 — THIS FIELD IS RETAINED BUT IS NO LONGER THE BROKER DEFINITION.
        =====================================================================================
        A broker is now a Contact on the Contact.Broker RECORD TYPE. This checkbox no longer
        decides anything. Nothing in production Apex, no SOQL predicate, and no lookup filter
        reads it any more.

        🔴 RECONCILED 2026-08-20 (code review, same day). "No lookup filter reads it any more"
        was FALSE when first written here: five <lookupFilter> blocks - Disposition__c.Broker__c,
        Broker_Assignment__c.Broker__c, Lease_Inquiry__c.Broker__c,
        BOV_Broker_Change__c.Outgoing_Broker__c and BOV_Broker_Change__c.Incoming_Broker__c -
        still tested Contact.Is_Broker__c = True despite this comment already declaring the
        move complete, and each one refused all 19 real brokers as a result (measured: 0 rows
        match Is_Broker__c = true, 19 match the record type). All five have now been repointed
        to Contact.RecordType.DeveloperName = 'Broker' in the same pass that added this note,
        so the sentence above is accurate again as of this fix - verify against a live grep of
        `Contact.Is_Broker__c` under objects/**/fields/*.field-meta.xml before trusting it a
        third time.

        WHY IT MOVED, MEASURED NOT ASSUMED. On usman-dpeg: ZERO Contacts carried
        Is_Broker__c = true, while 19 sat on the Contact.Broker record type and 6 on Master.
        Every `WHERE Is_Broker__c = true` query in the application was therefore matching
        NOTHING - broker options empty, Broker Hub empty, and every guest Broker-Portal
        submission silently defaulting to 'Standard' priority. That was an application-wide
        outage, not a per-feature bug, and repointing the definition is what repaired it.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 DO NOT DELETE, BACKFILL OR DE-PERMISSION THIS FIELD. RETIREMENT IS DEFERRED.
        =====================================================================================
        That is a user-confirmed scope boundary of the 2026-08-20 change, not an oversight.
        The field stays, unused, and is retired under its own separate task. Reasons to leave
        it alone until then:

          - TestDataFactory.createBrokerContacts STILL SETS IT, deliberately, and several test
            classes still resolve their fixture with `WHERE Is_Broker__c = true`. Deleting the
            field breaks those tests without touching a single production predicate.
          - classes/BrokerLookupFilterEnforcementTest depends on being able to set it to values
            that CONTRADICT the record type. That is how it proves the lookup filters read the
            record type and not this checkbox. Remove the field and that proof cannot be
            expressed at all.
          - The six broker <lookupFilter> blocks, the Broker_Required_On_Submission validation
            rule, ~40 force-ignored profiles and a long tail of prose all still name it.
            Retirement is a sweep, and a sweep done in the same change as a behavioural repoint
            is how a repoint gets blamed for a sweep's breakage.

        ⚠ IT IS NOT SAFE TO ASSUME THIS FIELD AND THE RECORD TYPE AGREE. They do not, and never
        did: the measurement above is 19 record-type brokers with ZERO ticked checkboxes. Do not
        write `RecordType.DeveloperName = 'Broker' AND Is_Broker__c = true` anywhere - that
        conjunction reintroduces the exact zero-row outage this change repaired. And do not
        write a backfill that ticks the box for the 19, either: that would make the two agree
        today and quietly re-establish the checkbox as a second, competing source of truth.

        SEE ALSO: classes/ContactSelector.cls (the four SOQL predicates that moved), and
        scripts/seed-broker-contacts.apex (whose destructive delete moved with them - it went
        from a guaranteed no-op to a real 19-row delete on the same day).
    -->
    <fullName>Is_Broker__c</fullName>
    <defaultValue>false</defaultValue>
    <description>RETAINED BUT NO LONGER THE BROKER DEFINITION (2026-08-20). A broker is a Contact on the Contact.Broker record type. Nothing reads this checkbox any more; retirement is a separate deferred task. Do not use it to identify brokers and do not combine it with the record type in a filter.</description>
    <label>Is Broker</label>
    <trackFeedHistory>false</trackFeedHistory>
    <type>Checkbox</type>
</CustomField>
